  "body": "I'm sure this has been discussed, but I was wondering why [the HTMLElement constructor](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#dom-htmlelement) step 2 checks for `new.target` specifically and does not consider the prototype chain (ie super constructors) as well.\n\nIf step 2 instead walked new.target's prototypes, and step 7 was updated, it would enable subclassing in JavaScript:\n\n``` javascript\nclass X extends HTMLElement {}\nwindow.customElements.define('my-x', X);\nclass Y extends X {\n  m() { console.log(this.localName); }\n}\nlet y = new Y();\ny.m(); // logs my-x\n```\n\nI&rsquo;m not sure this is that useful&mdash;elements minted by the parser will always be a defined type&mdash;but having stared at Blink bindings a bit I think we could support this added flexibility. Mostly because of the constraints ES6 classes impose on construction, prototype and constructor properties.\n",
